# A Simple, Transition Metal Catalyst‐Free Method for the Design of Complex Organic Building Blocks Used to Construct Porous Metal–Organic Frameworks

## abstract
The design of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) having large pore sizes and volumes often requires the use of complex organic ligands, currently synthesized using costly and time-consuming palladium-catalyzed coupling chemistry. Thus, in the present work, a new strategy for ligand design is reported, where piperazine and dihydrophenazine units are used as substitutes for benzene rings, which are the basic building block of most MOF ligands. This chemistry, which is based on simple, nucleophilic aromatic substitution (SN Ar) reactions, is used for the transition metal catalyst-free construction of 21 new, carboxylate-based ligands with varying sizes, shapes, and denticity and 15 linear di- and tetra-nitriles. Moreover, to demonstrate the utility of the ligands as building blocks, 16 new structurally diverse MOFs having surface areas up to 3100 m2 g-1 were also synthesized.

## Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), which consist of metalcontaining secondary building units (SBUs) that are interlinked by organic ligands via coordination bonds, are a highly recognized class of adsorbents due to record internal surface areas and the ease with which their pore structures can be precisely engineered.[1] Through the judicious selection of the organic and inorganic building blocks, one can control pore size, shape, and surface functionality, thus directing the formation of MOFs having anticipated properties and hence utility in a plethora of applications such as separations,[2] gas storage,[3] chemical conversion,[4] biomedical applications,[5] sensing,[6] etc.[7] While controlling the formation of a desired metal SBU is not always straightforward,[8] the predefined shape of the organic ligand is guaranteed, permitting a modular approach to MOF design and the easy introduction of multifunctional properties.[9] In the last 20 years, MOF studies have gravitated from the predominant use of small, commercially available ligands towards the design of larger, more complex derivatives with varying functionality.[7a,10] These efforts were spurred by the desire to obtain MOFs equipped with record-setting surface areas up to $7839\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Brunauer– Emmett–Teller, BET),[11] to acquire the pore sizes and volumes necessary to accommodate large guest species,[12] and to push beyond the existing structural limitations of the chemistry. Since, hundreds of MOF ligands having a host of different shapes, sizes, and/or connectivity[13] and a multitude of different functional groups were constructed, allowing one to choose or tune the properties of the resulting framework.[14]
While progress in ligand design has been nothing short of exciting, complex MOF ligands are often based on oligophenylene (OPL) units that feature $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-C}}$ bonds between two or more interconnected benzene rings (Scheme 1, b). Such structures require Pd-catalyzed Suzuki–Miyaura coupling reactions for their synthesis.[15] While such methods are reliable and well established, the costs of their multistep, time-consuming workups,[12c,d] combined with the use of expensive precious metal catalysts, could limit the further scale-up and hence, the implementation of resulting MOFs.[16] In addition, one cannot ignore the large quantities of solvent that are often consumed to produce a desired ligand with sufficient purity for subsequent MOF synthesis.[16a] Due to the lack of less costly alternatives, Pdcatalyzed coupling chemistry has remained the dominant approach to ligand synthesis throughout the MOF field to date.[12]
To eliminate the need for expensive precious metal catalysts and possibly simplify ligand reaction protocols, we explored strategies to alter the backbone of complex MOF ligands. We found, by replacing traditional $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-C}}$ bonds with $\mathrm{C-N}$ bonds, various OPL-like MOF ligands can be readily made (Scheme 1). In these new ligands, the $\mathrm{C-N}$ bonds are formed between an aromatic carbon and piperazine ring. While such chemistry can be done using Pd or Ni-catalyzed Buchwald–Hartwig amination reactions,[17] here it is done using transition metal-free nucleophilic aromatic substitution $(\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{N}}\mathbf{Ar})$ reactions.[18] The latter requires the presence of a strong electron-withdrawing group (EWG) on the aromatic ring to proceed;[19] thus, the initial building blocks must be chosen accordingly. Given this, we harnessed the EW nature of the carboxylate functionality, which is already required by default for the construction of carboxylatebased MOFs, as a plausible activator to drive $\mathbf{S}_{\mathbf{N}}\mathbf{Ar}$ reactions.[20] Further, stronger EWGs, such as nitrile groups (Scheme 1, a)[21] can instead be employed and converted to carboxylates after the construction of the ligand backbone for the purpose of subsequent MOF synthesis.[22] Notably, compared to their OPL counterparts, piperazine-based ligands are expected to have a higher charge density on the carboxylate due to the resonance stabilization effect caused by conjugation of the nitrogen lone pair with the EWG (Section S1, Scheme S1, Figure S1). So, it was hypothesized that piperazine ligands might offer stronger metal-ligand bonds, possibly boosting the stability of the resulting MOFs.[2g,23]
Despite the simplicity of the proposed ligand design, to the best of our knowledge, there are only three reported MOF ligands having a piperazine-based backbone. Of these, only one report used a similar $\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{N}}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{r}$ approach as explored here.[24] Unfortunately, the resulting framework was nonporous, requiring an additional bipyridine-based ligand to pin the pores open. This is presumably due to the flexibility of the 7-membered homopiperazine backbone, which likely induces strain, reducing framework stability. In addition, two other isostructural piperazine-ligands were previously made using metal-catalyzed coupling chemistry. In the first work, the ligand was used for the synthesis of a NbO-type Cu-MOF, which was subsequently assessed for methane storage,[25] and the second was used for the synthesis of an isostructural NbO type Cu-MOF that was employed in three-component coupling and Pechmann reactions.[26] Given that piperazine-based ligands are underexplored in the literature and the potential synthetic gains to be made by employing $\mathrm{C-N}$ coupling reactions, we developed a general methodology for the synthesis of a host of new MOF ligands that encompass different types of connectivity and varying functionality. Furthermore, we have demonstrated the additional synthesis of 16 MOFs with BET surface areas up to $3100\mathrm{{m}^{2}\mathrm{{g}^{-1}}}$ .
## Results and Discussion
To commence the study, the simplest member of the piperazine-based ligand series, Pip- $\mathbf{diCO_{2}H}$ was synthesized in two steps starting from 4-fluorobenzonitrile and piperazine. While the first step of this synthesis was already reported[27] with $83\%$ yield, it was improved herein to a $98\%$ yield $\mathrm{~7~g~}$ scale) of the Pip-diBN intermediate (Scheme 1, Section S3.1). The second hydrolysis step, performed for the first time in this study, resulted in the desired carboxylate, $\mathbf{Pip-diCO_{2}H}$ , with a $98\%$ yield on a $5\mathrm{g}$ scale (Scheme 1). Considering that the $\mathbf{Pip-diCO_{2}H}$ has a strong resemblance to p-terphenyl- $4,4^{\prime\prime}$ -dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{tpdc)}$ used in the synthesis of the highly stable zirconium-based MOF, denoted UiO-68,[28] it was hypothesized that a structurally related material might form under similar conditions. However, despite several trials, the targeted $Z\mathrm{r}$ MOF was not obtained from Pip-d $\mathbf{\Delta}\mathbf{\ddot{i}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{O}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ likely due to the ligand’s low solubility in common solvents (Section S3.6). Such issues are not uncommon in the MOF field and might be alleviated by engineering methyl substituents onto the ligand backbone.[12d] Inspired by this work, a similar strategy was employed here; the standard piperazine unit was substituted with trans-2,5-dimethylpiperazine, in hopes that the methyl functionality would enhance solubility. The first step of the synthetic process yielded the Dimepip-diBN intermediate in a $74\%$ yield on a $46{\mathrm{g}}$ scale (Section S3.1). The molecule was noticeably more soluble in DMSO-d6 compared to its unsubstituted analog, Pip-diBN, allowing its characterization via NMR. Moreover, this molecule was later crystallized from DMSO and assessed via single crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD) to better understand the effect that additional methyl groups have on the crystal structure of the dinitrile (Figure S2).
Next, the corresponding carboxylic acid, Dimepip$\mathbf{diCO_{2}H}$ , was synthesized with an $87\%$ yield on a $\mathbf{7}\:\mathbf{g}$ scale (Section S3.6) and subsequently used for the synthesis of a $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF, using L-proline as the modulator.[29] After fine-tuning the synthetic protocols and the MOF activation conditions, the first highly porous piperazine-based MOF, $\mathbf{z}_{\mathbf{r}}$ -dimepip, was realized with a BET surface area of $3100{\mathrm{m}}^{2}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ (Figure S3).[30] As expected, its structure strongly resembles that of UiO-68 (a Zr MOF built with H2tpdc)[28] as indicated by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD, Figure 1). Rietveld refinement of the PXRD pattern of the activated MOF reveals a match with the expected UiO-68 structure (Figures S4—S6),[31] and thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) and FTIR spectra reveal the expected composition, indicating a nearly defect-free MOF with only $2\%$ of the linkers missing (Figures S7, S8). The new MOF is stable after activation and in the air, as evidenced by the high-angle diffraction data (Figure 1).
After showcasing the possibility to construct a highly porous MOF using the new piperazine-based building block, the modular nature of the new synthetic approach was further exploited to test any limitations associated with varying the ligand structure. For this, various nitrile building blocks decorated with nitro-, amino- methyl-, trifluoromethyl-, and fluoro-functionality and also four different aliphatic and aromatic diamine cores were used to produce a total of 17 new ditopic ligands (Scheme 2). The syntheses of the ligands proceed smoothly in the conditions similar to those of pip- $\mathbf{diCO_{2}H}$ , with slight variations in temperature, solvent, and reaction time, which is dictated by the substituents. For certain nitriles, an alternative acid-catalyzed hydrolysis protocol was employed. Notably, the nitrofunctionalized ligands can be synthesized in water owed to the strong activation effect of the nitro group. The ligands were characterized via $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ , $^{19}\mathrm{F}$ , ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-}^{13}\mathrm{C}$ Nuclear magnetic spectroscopy (NMR), Fourier-Transformed Infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), High-resolution mass spectroscopy (HRMS), elemental analysis, and SCXRD (Section S3.1– S3.7, S4, S5, S6).
For several synthesized ligands and their nitrile precursors, namely, dimepip-diBN, DHP-diBN, (S)-mepip$\mathbf{diCO_{2}H}$ , dimepip- $({\bf2},{\bf2^{\prime}{\bf-F}}){\bf-d i C O}_{2}{\bf H}$ pip- $(2,2^{\prime}{\bf-C F}_{3}){\bf-d i C O}_{2}{\bf H},$ $\mathbf{DHP-diCO_{2}H}$ , single crystals were obtained, and their structures were determined by SCXRD (Table S1).[31] A linear structure was confirmed for all of the ligands as observed for the corresponding OPL analogs (Figures S8, S9). This observation is crucial as ligand linearity and geometrical parameters play a key role in the structure type and stability of the resulting MOF.[10a,32] The ligands were further employed for the construction of $10~\mathrm{Zr}$ MOFs (see protocols in the Supporting Information and Figures S10 to S53). Single crystals of two of the MOFs, including Zrdimepip- $(2\mathcal{,}2^{\prime}\mathcal{\cdot}\mathbf{F})$ and $\mathbf{Zr-DHP}$ , allowed structure determination via SCXRD (Figures S49, S50).[31] The MOFs indeed exhibited the classical UiO-type structure,[28,33] with 12- connected $\mathrm{[}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}\mathrm{]}^{12+}$ clusters bridged by the asprepared ligands. Resonance stabilization and conjugation effects were expectedly observed for the piperazine-, but not for the dihydrophenazine (DHP)-based ligands and MOFs (see Section S1 and Figure S52). The largest pore dimension, which is dictated by the ligand size, is 27.8556(3) $\mathring\mathbf{A}$ for $\mathbf{z}_{\mathbf{r}}.$ - dimepip- $(2,2^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathbf{F})$ and $27.{\overset{\cdot}{6204(2)}}{\overset{\cdot}{\mathbf{A}}}$ for $\mathbf{z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{P}$ ; this was determined by measuring the distance between oxygen atoms of $Z\mathrm{r}$ clusters across the octahedral pore. Further, to obtain more insight into the pore properties, the $\mathrm{Zeo++}$ software[34] was also used to determine the dimensions of the largest spherical guest molecule that can fit in the MOF pores (Table S4).
The relatively low BET surface area $(417{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ of $\mathbf{z}_{\mathbf{r}}$ - DHP is explained by the flexibility of the DHP ligand, which leads to a partial collapse of the MOF structure (Figure S53, bottom; Table S2). This is indicated by a drastic decrease in the unit cell parameter from 32.6790(3) Å to $25.26(3)\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ and peak broadening after solvent exchange and activation. Remarkably, the MOF structure can be recovered after soaking in DMF overnight, which led to a highly crystalline material and a unit cell parameter of $32.{\overset{\cdot}{7}}0{\overset{\cdot}{7}}(1){\overset{\cdot}{\mathrm{A}}}$ (Figure S53, center; Table S2). It was hypothesized that the flexibility of the $\mathbf{DHP-diCO_{2}H}$ ligand was a plausible explanation for the low porosity of the resulting $\mathbf{Zr-DHP}$ MOF. So, efforts were made to modify $\mathbf{DHP-diCO_{2}H}$ to minimize its flexibility. For this, a tetramethyl-substituted analog, DHP-(3,5,3’,5’-Me)- $\mathbf{diCO_{2}H}$ (Scheme 2, bottom right), was synthesized using a similar synthetic pathway. The corresponding activated MOF, $\mathbf{z}_{\mathbf{r}}$ -DHP-( ${\bf3},{\bf5},{\bf3}^{\prime},{\bf5}^{\prime}{\bf-M e})$ , expectedly has a similar structure as the solvated $\mathbf{z}_{\mathbf{r}}$ -DHP as evidenced by a LeBail fit of the PXRD pattern (Figure S23). Notably, the methyl substituted material has some flexibility upon solvent exchange and activation, as evidenced by PXRD (Figures S29, Table S2). Further, an ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption measurement (at $77\mathrm{K}$ ) indicates a breathing behavior. Despite this, the BET surface area of $\mathbf{Zr}$ -DHP-(3,5,3’,5’-Me) is $1800{\mathrm{m}}^{2}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ (Figure S31, S33), a value that is over four times that of $\mathbf{Zr-DHP}$ , indicating that the methyl groups on the ligand backbone indeed improve framework rigidity and prevent pore collapse. Such observations warrant future studies of DHP-based materials for flexibility-enabled applications.
The broad tunability offered by varying the functional groups on the ligand backbones is expected to enable interesting applications for the resulting MOFs. For instance, chiral (S)-piperazine ligands, like (S)-mepip- $\mathbf{dico_{2}H}$ (Scheme 2, top left), might lead to applications in chiral separations and/or enantioselective catalysis.[2f,35] Also, fluorinated building blocks reportedly instill hydrophobicity and adsorption selectivity in MOFs,[36] while nitro groups might undergo electron transfer interactions with the piperazine, enabling enhanced light absorption, fluorescence, and/or photocatalysis.[37] Notably, piperazine units were previously reported to provide luminescent properties to MOFs and polymers.[38] Also, ligands based on the aromatic DHP unit, might lead to interesting photoactivity, redox activity, luminescence and battery applications.[39] Moreover, ligands decorated with amines can allow the post-synthetic modifications of the resulting MOFs,[4c,40] and ligands that lead to dynamic breathing behaviors in MOF structures, such as $\mathbf{z}_{\mathbf{r}}.$ - pip-(3,3’-Me), Zr-DHP, and $\mathbf{z}_{\mathbf{r}}$ -DHP- $(3,5,3^{\prime},5^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathbf{Me})$ , can lead to MOFs having highly selective gas adsorption behavior and high adsorption capacities.[41]
Thus far, the work offers several novelties. For example, to the best of our knowledge, all of the ditopic carboxylate ligands reported here are new, except DHP-diCO2H;[42] however, the reported synthesis notably employed Pdcatalyzed coupling chemistry and featured lower yields and time consuming chromatographic workups.[42] The majority of the corresponding dinitrile precursors are also new except pip-diBN[27] and DHP-diBN.[43] Further, using this new $\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{N}}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{r}$ methodology, no purification, other than washing with water, ethanol, or acetone, was needed. Inexpensive starting materials were also used, and no transition metal catalysts were employed.
Having shown the formation of a MOF series having structures similar to UiO-68, the limitations of the synthetic methodology were further tested by varying ligand geometries. For this, several piperazine-based tetracarboxylate ligands were synthesized. Tetrarcarboxylate-based MOFs, which have been extensively studied to date, offer exciting performance in applications coupled to the methane and hydrogen storage.[25,26,44] The first ligand in the present study was made by changing the nitrile building block from 4- fluorobenzonitrile (Scheme 1a) to 5-fluoroisophthalonitrile (Scheme 3a), yielding the production of a tetranitrile piptetraBN $89\%$ yield, Section S3), which was readily converted to the desired ligand, pip-tetra $\mathbf{CO_{2}H}$ ( $81\%$ yield). Given that this ligand was reported once before and used in MOF construction,[25] the tunability of the new approach was demonstrated through the additional synthesis of two new ligands, namely dimepip-tetra $\mathbf{CO_{2}H}$ (Scheme 3b) and DHP-tetra $\mathbf{CO_{2}H}$ (Scheme 3c). Next, an isostructural series of $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOFs having the NbO type structure[25] were also successfully made, as determined by SCXRD (Figures S55, S56, Table S1).[31] The as-prepared MOF samples are pure as determined via PXRD (Figure S57). Also, upon activation, the Cu-tetra-dimepip framework was found to have a BET surface area of $2289{\mathrm{m}}^{2}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ (Figure S58); this value is comparable to the ones reported for an isostructural piperazine-based analog, NJU-Bai-19 (ligand $\c=$ piptetra $\mathbf{CO_{2}H}$ , BET surface area $2803{\mathrm{m}}^{2}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1})$ ,[25] which features no methyl groups on the ligand backbone, and other isostructural OPL analogs including NOTT-101 (ligand $\c=$ $[1,1^{\prime}{:}4^{\prime},1^{\prime\prime}$ -terphenyl]- $-3,3^{\prime\prime},5,5^{\prime\prime}$ -tetracarboxylic acid, BET surface area $2316\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1},$ ), NOTT-106 (ligand $\Longrightarrow2^{\prime}\mathcal{S}^{\prime}$ -dimethyl$[1,1^{\prime}{:}4^{\prime},1^{\prime\prime}$ -terphenyl]- $^{-3,3^{\prime\prime},5,5^{\prime\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylic acid, BET surface area $1855\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1\cdot}$ ), and NOTT-107 (ligand $=2^{\prime},3^{\prime},5^{\prime},6^{\prime}.$ - tetramethyl- $[1,1^{\prime}{:}4^{\prime},1^{\prime\prime}$ -terphenyl]- $3{,}3^{\prime\prime}{,}5{,}5^{\prime\prime}$ -tetracarboxylic acid, BET surface area $1822\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ .[45] Unfortunately, Cutetra-DHP has a relatively low BET surface area of $320{\mathrm{m}}^{2}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ (Figure S58), while the BET surface area of its anthracene analog, PCN-14, is $1753\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .[46] This could stem from the flexibility of the DHP-based molecules, as discussed above for the $\mathbf{Zr}$ -DHP MOF.[47]
Given the ease of incorporating nitro, halogens, trifluoromethyl, or methyl groups onto the ligand backbone, next, efforts were made to design ligands having hydroxyl functionality that is ortho to the carboxylate group. Such ligands are known to lead to one of the most reputable MOF families known as MOF-74 or CPO-27.[12d] These MOFs consist of metal oxide chains that are connected by OPL ligands forming a hexagonal array of one-dimensional channels and a high density of accessible metal sites. These structural features make the MOF-74 series useful in a host of applications ranging from separations to catalysis.[12d,48] However, to obtain such ligands, an appropriate synthetic pathway had to be developed. It was previously shown that when halogens are found in ortho- positions to the nitrile groups, they can be converted to hydroxyl functionality via treatment with a suitable alkali metal hydroxide.[49] Using this strategy, a piperazine-based molecule having pendant $-\mathrm{F}$ next to $-C\mathbf{N}$ groups was first constructed, pip- $(2,2^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathbf{F})$ - diBN, (Scheme 4, Section S3.1) in a $65\%$ yield. Next, the F functionality was converted to $-\mathrm{OH}$ using solid NaOH under anhydrous conditions. This process afforded the complete removal of fluorine from the structure forming the desired dihydroxy intermediate, pip- $({\bf2},{\bf2^{\prime}{\bf.O H}})$ -diBN (Scheme 4, Section S3.5) in an $89\%$ yield. In the last step, the nitrile groups were hydrolyzed to obtain the desired novel ligand, pip- $\mathbf{(2,2^{\prime}{\cdot}O H){\cdot}d i C O_{2}H}$ (Scheme 4) in an $82\%$ yield and on a $\boldsymbol{13}\mathrm{g}$ scale.
Next, efforts were made to employ the new ligand to construct an isoreticular series of MOF-74 analogs. Surprisingly, no solvothermal reactions carried out in dimethylformamide (DMF)-containing mixtures yielded a crystalline product, even after trying the synthesis with a variety of metals, such as $\mathbf{Mg}^{\mathrm{II}}$ , $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ , $\mathbf{Ni}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ , and $Z\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ . This finding is contrary to most existing studies focused on the synthesis of MOF-74 analogs.[12d,50] Thus, other, previously reported reaction conditions were also explored. For instance, Fjellvåg et al. employed a $\mathrm{THF/H_{2}O}$ mixture at a high temperature in a Teflon-lined autoclave,[48a,51] while Díaz et al[52] and Maspoch et al.[53] used aqueous solutions of a NaOH-deprotonated ligand to form MOF-74 analogs based on the 2,5-dihydroxyterepthalate ligand. After slightly tweaking these aforementioned protocols, the conditions necessary to form crystalline analogs of MOF-74 using various metal salts including $\mathrm{Co^{II},N i^{I I}}$ , and $Z\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{\scriptscriptstyleII}}$ (Figure S60) were elucidated. The PXRD patterns of the corresponding MOFs were found to have a relatively good match with the simulated pattern of the Mg-IRMOF-74-III,[12d] a structure that contains a ligand that is similar to the pip- $(2,2^{\prime}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{OH})$ - $\mathbf{diCO_{2}H}$ , but is instead constructed from three benzene units. As expected, TEM microscopy (Figure S62, b-d, Figure S63) indicates that the MOF crystals are sub-micronsized rods, and there are noticeable mesoporous channels inside the structure of ${\bf{Z}}{\bf{n}}$ -pip- $(2,2^{\prime}{\bf-O H})$ . While such observations are in line with those made in previous reports on mesoporous MOF-74 analogs,[12d,54] the BET surface areas of the piperazine-based materials are only moderate, with values of 400, 230, and $110\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the Zn-, Ni- and Copip- $(2,2^{\prime}{\bf-O H})$ , respectively (Figure S61, a). These values are significantly lower than that of Mg-IRMOF-74-III, $2440\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .[12d] TGA analysis revealed (Figure S63, b) an increased organics content in these MOF materials, and a shoulder at $\approx1650{\ c m}^{-1}$ is visible in both the ligand and MOF FTIR spectra (Figure S64). This might indicate that a significant amount of ligand remains in the MOF pores after washing. Reportedly, the sample quality of mesoporousMOF-74-based analogs can be dependent on the activation procedure,[53,54] and intergrowth of the crystalline phases can lead to reduced surface areas as well.[55] While these preliminary results indicate that the formation of various IRMOF-74-III analogs is possible with the pip- $(2,2^{\prime}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{OH})$ - $\mathbf{diCO_{2}H}$ ligand, future optimization of the MOF synthesis and post-synthetic treatment is necessary to address their low porosity.
Given the ease of the reactions described thus far and the successful design of many different dicarboxylate and tetracarboxylate ligands, questions were raised as to whether this protocol could be extended to the design of triangular tricarboxylate and hexacarboxylate ligands. In particular, hexacarboxylates, combined with copper, give rise to a series of reputable MOFs having an rht-topology. These materials feature high stability and offer outstanding gas separation and storage properties.[56] In theory, a single electron-withdrawing group on a benzene ring can activate three positions, namely two ortho- and one para-, for $\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{N}}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{r}$ reactions.[57] Thus, it was hypothesized that using trifluorobenzene that contains a strong electron-withdrawing group, such as nitro, would lead to the desired piperazine intermediates necessary for the subsequent synthesis of triand hexacarboxylate ligands. To start, piperazine-carboxylate precursors were first synthesized (Scheme 5) from the corresponding commercially available aromatic amines[58] to obtain the known 4-pip- $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{Et}\mathbf{\cdotH}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{I}^{[59]}$ and the new 5-pip$\mathbf{dicO}_{2}\mathbf{Me}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}$ (Section S3.8). The protocols are simple and require minimal workup, affording the HCl salts of the corresponding piperazine-carboxylate precursor in $54\mathrm{-}81\%$ yield on $24{-}63\ \mathrm{g}$ scales. The resulting salts are readily deprotonated in situ by ${\bf K}_{2}\bf C O_{3}$ producing the free amine species, which directly participate in the $\mathbf{S}_{\mathbf{N}}\mathbf{Ar}$ reaction. The subsequent hydrolysis afforded the new triangular ligands, referred to as tris- $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ and hexa- $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ in a $78\mathrm{-}80\%$ yield (Scheme 5).
Next, the hexa- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ ligand was employed for the synthesis of a $\mathtt{C u}$ -based MOF, yielding yellow-green single crystals of the new Cu-hexa- $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ MOF (Figure S65). SCXRD analysis expectedly revealed a structure similar to that of NOTT-112,[56c] which features an rht topology that is constructed from copper paddlewheels that are interlinked by hexa- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ ligands (Figures S65, S66).[31] Moreover, the experimental PXRD pattern of Cu-hexa- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ matched the calculated one (Figure S67, a). The measured BET surface area was $1990{\mathrm{m}}^{2}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ (Figure S67, b). While this confirms accessible pores in the MOF, TGA, and FTIR (Figures S68, S69) data may indicate that residual ligand and/or other amorphous impurities are present in the sample. This could explain the lower BET surface area of Cu-hexa- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ when compared to the isostructural NOTT-112 $(3800{\mathrm{m}}^{2}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1})$ . Thus, while the hexa- $\mathbf{\cdotNO}_{2}$ ligand can be readily employed for the design of new porous materials, future efforts are needed to optimize their surface areas. This could be achieved via optimization of the synthetic and subsequent workup procedures, such as further washing and/or supercritical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ activation, etc.[60]
With the successful isolation of two triangular ligands and construction of a new MOF, Cu-hexa- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ , a further expansion of the ligand series was considered. It was thought that construction of tetrangular ligands should be possible with a correct choice of the central building unit. The resulting tetragonal ligands (Scheme 6) are meant to mimic the known OPL octacarboxylate ligands, such as $4^{\prime\prime},5^{\prime\prime}$ - bis( $_{3^{\prime},5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxy-[1,1’-biphenyl]-4-yl)-
$[1,1^{\prime};4^{\prime},1^{\prime\prime};2^{\prime\prime},1^{\prime\prime\prime};4^{\prime\prime\prime},1^{\prime\prime\prime\prime}$ -quinquephenyl]- $^{3,3^{\prime\prime\prime\prime}},5,5^{\prime\prime\prime\prime}$ -tetracarboxylic acid, which are synthesized using Pd-catalysed chemistry and employed for the construction of MFM MOFs that are known for their remarkable $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage capacities.[61] Notably, tetrafluoro-1,4-benzoquinone was found to possess the necessary reactivity for the construction of the targeted ligand, octa-BQ (Scheme 6).
While the synthetic yield is low, $13\%$ , and the NMR spectra of the final product are complicated (Figure S170– S172, Section S3.9), the formation of this molecule is undoubtedly confirmed via high-resolution mass spectrometry (MS, Figure S182). The most intense peaks in the MS data correspond to the target molecule in two different ionized states, and current efforts are aimed at optimizing the ligand yield and subsequent MOF synthesis.
After making many different piperazine and DHPligands, they were compared to similar OPL analogs (Figure S183, S184, Table S5). In general, the new ligands offer lower cost, by up to 17.6 times, than OPLs. To date, while only the cost of the starting materials used in gram-scale ligand production was considered, the post-synthetic workup and optimized, large-scale syntheses could be considered in the future. Notably, the piperazine and DHP-ligands require no purification beyond simple washing (no column chromatography); so, they are likely to be even more cost effective than observed here. Further, the new ligands require no critical metal catalysts, unlike the OPLs, making this process more sustainable. Last, the new ligands allow the construction of many MOFs for which there are no known OPL analogs, providing numerous new frameworks to explore.
Given the added value of the new ligands, attention was also paid to possibly synthesizing other classes of porous materials, such as porous organic polymers (POPs). For this, imine-linked POPs were chosen as a model example owed to their stability and ease of synthesis.[62] Next, 4-fluorobenzaldehyde was reacted with piperazine to provide a piperazine-based linear building block, pip-diCHO (Scheme S2, Figure S185) in $38.3\%$ yield. Subsequently, it was combined with 1,3,5-tris(4-aminophenyl)triazine (TAPT, Figure S98) to produce a porous polymer, pip-diCHO-TAPT, having a BET surface area of $345\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Figure S186, S187). This result indicates the suitability of the new methodology to produce different types of ligands (aldehyde) and other classes of porous materials beyond MOFs.
## Conclusion
In summary, a new concept for the design of nitirle- and carboxylate-based ligands having a piperazine-like core is presented. This new methodology, which does not involve transition metal catalysts and requires minimal purification (other than washing), has allowed the synthesis of 21 novel carboxylate-based ligands (on scales up to $\displaystyle{13\mathrm{~g}},$ and 15 linear di- and tetra-nitriles (on scales up to $\mathsf{46}\:\mathrm{g}$ ). Further, the ligand backbones can be decorated with a host of varying functionality (such as aliphatic piperazines, aromatic dihydrophenazines, and side $-\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , $-\mathrm{F}$ , $-\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ , $-\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ groups), and the ligand shape, size, and denticity can be readily modified. Further, the simple, cheap synthetic protocols of the piperazine-based ligands, combined with their rather linear confirmations, make them an attractive alternative to the standard oligophenylene ligands that are often used as MOF building blocks. Thus, 16 new structurally robust piperazine-based MOFs, which offer structures that are similar to some of the most reputable oligophenylene-based MOF families reported in the field, were also synthesized.
Given the observed versatility of this new approach to ligand design, we believe it can be easily extended to other structurally diverse ligands in the future. For instance, longer piperazine-based blocks such as $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipiperidine and 1,4-bis(piperazino)benzene[63] can be employed for ligand construction, which will allow the dimensions of the ligands to be expanded similar to oligophenylene chemistry. Further, we envision ligands having different connectivity (other than nitriles, carboxylic acids and aldehydes). For instance, the dinitriles are known to react with sodium azide to produce bis-tetrazole ligands that can be employed for the production of highly stable tetrazolate MOFs.[64] Moreover, it is thought that this chemistry could serve as a platform, not only for the design of highly porous MOFs, but could also be readily extended to the design of other porous framework materials, such as COFs (covalent organic frameworks),[65] HOFs (Hydrogen-bonded organic frameworks) and POPs (porous organic polymers), as illustrated with a POP example made in this study. It is hoped, given the ease of the synthetic methodology, its potential scalability, and the reduction in time and cost (compared to alloligophenylene counterparts, Figure S180, S181, Table S5)), that this chemistry might provide easier and cheaper access to large-pore materials and hence, help open doors for their future implementation into a variety of applications relevant to host–guest chemistry.
